Z||000005||jelly2m8||12-23-2001||12:49 PM||||No the 4 cylinder slave bracket won't work on a V6, unless you  modify it heavily.<P> Try Ed parks, or Bob parker for the part. Rodneys price of 75 bux isn't that bad, seeing this part is getting harder to get.<P> If you have all the pieces to your old bracket, you should be able to have it welded back together. Find a welding shop that can weld aluminum.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||12-23-2001||12:55 PM||||nevermind<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||jelly2m8||12-23-2001||01:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>The 4 cyl 4 speed slave bracket is the same as the V6 4 speed slave bracket. Both 5 speed brackets are different. You didn't give enough info.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry Rockcrawl, your info is wrong. The 4 cyl / 4 spd combination was only available in the 84 model year. The slave bracket on those are made of steel and resemble a piece of angle iron.<P> The V6 / 4 spd slave bracket, which I believe what he is looking for is made from aluminum, and is totally different  than the steel 84 one.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||12-23-2001||02:36 PM||||I did what jelly2m8 suggested, to the slave bracket on my 1986 V6 SE 4-speed.  In my case, it broke just one place (i.e., into 2 pieces): the little thin flange that attaches to the shift cable bracket with a clip nut.  Took it to an oldtime welding shop in downtown Wichita (where they actually have skill & brains).  Not to name names, but I'm sure to Wichitans it would ring a Bell  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>The fillet radius at the location where it had broken was <B>very</B> small.  So, I asked them to add lots of material into that junction between the little flange and the beefy part of the bracket (as much as possible w/o having the attach bolt ride the radius), as well as on the opposite side, up to the height of the little rectangular boss around the bolt hole.  In fact, they suggested the very things I had in my mind when I went in there (I told you they have brains  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).<P>Anyway, they charged the minimum: $25. I'm pleased with the result.  Your actual cost may vary  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <P>BTW, I also considered riveting a steel angle onto the side of the bracket, to replace the flange that broke off.  You might want to consider that option also.||||reg||1
Z||000009||firstfiero||12-23-2001||03:01 PM||||   Thanks guys... I knew you would come through! I'll talk to him monday and if he still can't find one I'll give ed parks a buzz. Thanks guys.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000010||Rodney||12-23-2001||03:44 PM||||Rodneys price of 75 bux isn't that bad, seeing this part is getting harder to get.<P>My reproduction steel 4 speed slave bracket is $56. My reproduction shift/slave bracket for the Getrag is $75. ||||reg||1
